.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH git-workon 1  "git-workon 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
git\-workon \- A git plugin for managing worktrees
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgit\-workon\fR [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR]... [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-dirty\fR] [\fB\-\-clean\fR] [\fB\-\-ahead\fR] [\fB\-\-behind\fR] [\fB\-\-gone\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fINAME\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
An opinionated git worktree workflow for managing multiple branches simultaneously.
.PP
git\-workon clones repositories as bare repos with a worktrees\-first layout, then provides commands for creating, finding, and cleaning up worktrees — so switching between branches is just `cd`, not `git stash && git checkout`.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase logging verbosity
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Decrease logging verbosity
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output results as JSON
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable color output
.TP
\fB\-\-dirty\fR
Show only worktrees with uncommitted changes
.TP
\fB\-\-clean\fR
Show only worktrees without uncommitted changes
.TP
\fB\-\-ahead\fR
Show only worktrees with unpushed commits
.TP
\fB\-\-behind\fR
Show only worktrees behind their upstream
.TP
\fB\-\-gone\fR
Show only worktrees whose upstream branch is deleted
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-interactive\fR
Disable interactive mode (for testing/scripting)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fINAME\fR]
A partial name of a worktree
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
git\-workon\-clone(1)
Perform a bare clone of a repository and create an initial worktree
.TP
git\-workon\-copy\-untracked(1)
Copy any untracked files in <from> to <to>
.TP
git\-workon\-doctor(1)
Detect and repair workspace issues
.TP
git\-workon\-find(1)
Find a worktree to work on
.TP
git\-workon\-init(1)
Create a new bare repository and an initial worktree
.TP
git\-workon\-list(1)
List worktrees
.TP
git\-workon\-move(1)
Rename a worktree and its branch atomically
.TP
git\-workon\-new(1)
Create a new worktree
.TP
git\-workon\-prune(1)
Prune stale worktrees
.TP
git\-workon\-shell\-init(1)
Generate shell integration script (wrapper function + completions)
.TP
git\-workon\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.SS "Clone a repository"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon clone https://github.com/owner/repo
cd repo/main        # jumps into the default branch worktree
.EE
.RE
.SS "Initialize a new repository"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon init                  # bare init in current directory with initial worktree
git workon init my-project       # bare init in my-project/ with initial worktree
.EE
.RE
.SS "Create a new worktree"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon new                   # interactive: prompts for name, then base branch
git workon new my-feature        # creates branch + worktree
git workon new my-feature --base main   # branch from main
git workon new my-feature --orphan      # create branch with no parent commits
git workon new my-feature --detach      # detach HEAD in the new worktree
git workon #123                  # create worktree from PR #123 (auto-fetches)
.EE
.RE
.SS "Find an existing worktree"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon find                  # interactive: fuzzy picker across all worktrees
git workon find main             # prints path to the 'main' worktree
git workon my-feature            # shorthand for find (prints path)
                                 # note: multiple fuzzy matches also trigger the picker
.EE
.RE
.SS "List worktrees"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon list                  # all worktrees
git workon list --dirty          # only worktrees with uncommitted changes
git workon list --ahead          # worktrees with unpushed commits
git workon list --gone           # worktrees whose upstream branch was deleted
.EE
.RE
.SS "Prune stale worktrees"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon prune                 # interactive: shows candidates, prompts for confirmation
git workon prune --yes           # skip confirmation prompt (for scripting)
git workon prune --dry-run       # preview without deleting
git workon prune my-feature      # prune a specific worktree
git workon prune --gone          # also prune worktrees whose upstream branch is gone
git workon prune --merged        # also prune worktrees merged into the default branch
.EE
.RE
.SS "Rename a worktree"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon move new-name                # rename the current worktree
git workon move old-name new-name       # rename a specific worktree
git workon move new-name --dry-run      # preview without renaming
.EE
.RE
.SS "Diagnose workspace issues"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon doctor                # check for broken worktrees and missing dependencies
git workon doctor --fix          # automatically repair fixable issues
git workon doctor --dry-run      # preview fixes without applying
.EE
.RE
.SS "Copy untracked files between worktrees"
.RS 4
.EX
git workon copy-untracked main my-feature --pattern '.env*'
.EE
.RE
.SH "SHELL INTEGRATION"
\fBgit workon find\fR and \fBgit workon new\fR print the worktree path to stdout — they don't \fBcd\fR on their own, since a subprocess can't change the parent shell's directory. Without the shell wrapper, you'd need to do this yourself:
.RS 4
.EX
# bash / zsh
cd "$(git workon find main)"

# fish
cd (git workon find main)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The shell integration sets up a \fBworkon\fR wrapper function that captures the output and \fBcd\fRs automatically when the result is a directory:
.RS 4
.EX
# bash
eval "$(git workon shell-init bash)"

# zsh
eval "$(git workon shell-init zsh)"

# fish
git workon shell-init fish | source
.EE
.RE
.PP
After setup, \fBworkon <name>\fR changes your current directory to the worktree, and \fBworkon new <name>\fR drops you directly into the newly created worktree.
.PP
> **Scripting**: pass \fB--no-interactive\fR to \fBfind\fR and \fBnew\fR, and \fB--yes\fR to \fBprune\fR, to suppress all prompts.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
\fBgit-workon\fR uses git config keys under the \fBworkon.*\fR namespace:
.RS 4
.EX
[workon]
    defaultBranch = main
    postCreateHook = npm install
    copyPattern = .env.local
    autoCopyUntracked = true
    pruneProtectedBranches = main
    pruneProtectedBranches = release/*
    prFormat = pr-{number}
.EE
.RE
.PP
See \fBman git-workon\fR or \fBgit workon --help\fR for full documentation.
